---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc480.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Applicability",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Mandatorily Redeemable Financial Instruments",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Obligations to Repurchase Shares by Transferring Assets",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Obligations to Issue a Variable Number of Shares",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Puttable Shares",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Temporary (Mezzanine) Equity — a Distinctly US Third Category",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "The Fixed-for-Fixed Principle",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Worked Example — Mandatorily Redeemable Preferred Stock",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Worked Example — Mezzanine Equity Presentation",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "Disclosure Requirements",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "ASC 480 vs IAS 32 — Comprehensive Comparison",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s15",
          label: "Related Topics",
        },
        {
          id: "s16",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Legal form as "shares" doesn't guarantee equity classification</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">An instrument LABELLED as "stock" or "shares" is not automatically EQUITY for accounting purposes — what matters is its SUBSTANCE: does it embody an unconditional obligation to transfer assets, or a variable, non-fixed settlement amount? ASC 480 ensures instruments with genuinely liability-like characteristics are classified as LIABILITIES, regardless of their legal form or label, echoing the Conceptual Framework's substance-over-form orientation.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">A foundational, heavily tested classification Topic</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 480 (alongside ASC 815-40's related indexation guidance) governs one of the most consequential classification questions in all of accounting — since liability versus equity classification affects leverage ratios, EPS, and covenant compliance. Its comparison to IAS 32 (Section 13) is a classic area of study, anchored by the genuinely US-specific "mezzanine equity" concept (Section 7).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Financial instruments with both debt and equity characteristics</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 480 applies to freestanding financial instruments that have characteristics of BOTH liabilities and equity — most significantly mandatorily redeemable instruments, obligations to repurchase shares, and certain obligations to issue a variable number of shares.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Mandatorily Redeemable Financial Instruments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A liability, despite its "equity" label</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An instrument embodying an UNCONDITIONAL obligation to REDEEM it by transferring assets at a SPECIFIED or DETERMINABLE date, or upon an event CERTAIN to occur, is classified as a <strong>LIABILITY</strong> — even if the instrument is legally structured as "preferred stock" or another equity-labelled security. This closely parallels IAS 32's own "contractual obligation to deliver cash" test, which similarly classifies mandatorily redeemable preferred shares as liabilities — a genuinely converged core principle for the most straightforward cases.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Obligations to Repurchase Shares by Transferring Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A liability, regardless of who holds the option</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An instrument obligating the ISSUER to repurchase its own shares by TRANSFERRING assets — such as a forward purchase contract on the issuer's own stock, or certain written put options on the issuer's own shares — is similarly classified as a LIABILITY, measured typically at the present value of the amount to be paid.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Obligations to Issue a Variable Number of Shares</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Broadly converged with IAS 32's own "fixed-for-fixed" logic</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An obligation to ISSUE a VARIABLE number of shares — where the monetary value of the obligation is FIXED (or tied to something OTHER than the issuer's own share price) rather than the SHARE COUNT itself being fixed — is classified as a LIABILITY. This is broadly converged in substance with IAS 32's own "fixed-for-fixed" test (Section 8): an instrument is EQUITY only if it will be settled by delivering a FIXED number of shares for a FIXED amount of consideration; anything indexed to a VARIABLE monetary amount generally fails equity classification under BOTH frameworks.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Puttable Shares</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Two different frameworks for the same underlying problem</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Shares the HOLDER can require the ISSUER to redeem ("puttable" instruments) generally fall into LIABILITY classification, or into the US-specific MEZZANINE EQUITY category (Section 7), under US GAAP/SEC guidance. <strong>IAS 32</strong> similarly classifies MOST puttable instruments as liabilities by default, but contains a NARROW "PUTTABLE INSTRUMENT EXCEPTION" — permitting EQUITY classification for certain instruments (e.g. some partnership or cooperative interests) that are the MOST SUBORDINATED class, share PRO RATA in net assets upon liquidation, and meet several other specific conditions. This is a NARROWER, more precisely defined exception than the broader US "mezzanine equity" presentation approach.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Temporary (Mezzanine) Equity — a Distinctly US Third Category</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A structural divergence with no IAS 32 counterpart</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">For instruments REDEEMABLE at the holder's option, or upon a CONTINGENT event OUTSIDE the issuer's control, that do NOT meet ASC 480's strict liability classification criteria but also aren't fully unconditional PERMANENT equity, US practice (originating from SEC Regulation S-X, later incorporated into the Codification) uses a THIRD presentation category: <strong>TEMPORARY or MEZZANINE EQUITY</strong> — presented on the Balance Sheet BETWEEN total liabilities and permanent shareholders' equity, outside of both. <strong>IAS 32 has NO equivalent third category</strong> — under IFRS, EVERY instrument is classified as EITHER a liability OR equity, full stop, with no intermediate presentation tier. This is one of the most STRUCTURALLY distinctive, genuinely American features of the entire liability/equity classification landscape.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>The Fixed-for-Fixed Principle</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Broadly converged, applied through ASC 815-40's own complex indexation guidance</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">A contract that will be settled by delivering a FIXED number of the issuer's OWN shares in exchange for a FIXED amount of cash or other consideration is generally treated as EQUITY (or equity-classified), consistent with IAS 32's fixed-for-fixed principle. This determination, under US GAAP, works through ASC 815-40's own detailed (and famously complex) indexation and equity classification guidance — a separate, adjacent body of rules that intersects heavily with ASC 480's own classification framework.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Mandatorily Redeemable Preferred Stock</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A liability despite the "preferred stock" label</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Preferred stock redeemable in 10 years</div><div class="wk-body">An entity issues $5,000,000 of preferred stock that MUST be redeemed by the issuer for cash in exactly 10 years — an unconditional, certain-to-occur redemption event.</div><div class="wk-answer">Despite being legally structured and labelled as "preferred STOCK," this instrument is classified as a <strong>LIABILITY</strong> under ASC 480, since it embodies an unconditional obligation to transfer $5,000,000 of assets at a specified future date. Dividends paid on the instrument are treated as INTEREST EXPENSE, not as a distribution of equity.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Mezzanine Equity Presentation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A third balance sheet category, between liabilities and equity</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Preferred stock redeemable at the holder's option after a contingent event</div><div class="wk-body">An entity issues preferred stock that the HOLDER may put back to the issuer for redemption only if a specified future acquisition or change-of-control event occurs — an event OUTSIDE the issuer's control, and not certain to occur.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since redemption is NOT unconditionally certain (it depends on a contingent event outside the issuer's control), this instrument does NOT meet ASC 480's strict liability classification — but because the holder COULD force redemption, it also isn't fully unconditional PERMANENT equity. The instrument is presented as <strong>TEMPORARY (MEZZANINE) EQUITY</strong>, on its own line between total liabilities and permanent shareholders' equity — a presentation option with NO equivalent under IAS 32, which would force a binary liability-or-equity classification for the SAME instrument instead.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 480 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature and terms of financial instruments classified as liabilities under this Topic</span><span>Nature and terms of instruments classified as temporary (mezzanine) equity, and the redemption features triggering that classification</span><span>Settlement alternatives, and the entity's accounting policy for classifying instruments with debt and equity characteristics</span></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a redemption event is genuinely "certain to occur," or merely contingent, driving liability vs mezzanine classification</span><span>Whether settlement terms genuinely satisfy the fixed-for-fixed principle</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Redemption value for instruments classified in temporary equity, which may require periodic remeasurement toward the redemption amount</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 480 vs IAS 32 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 480 vs IAS 32</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 480)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 32)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Mandatorily redeemable instruments</span><span>Liability</span><span>Liability — fully converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Fixed-for-fixed principle</span><span>Applied via ASC 815-40's own detailed indexation guidance</span><span>Applied directly within IAS 32 itself</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Puttable instrument exception</span><span>No equivalent narrow exception; addressed via mezzanine equity instead</span><span>A NARROW exception permits equity classification for certain most-subordinated, pro-rata puttable interests</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Third balance sheet category (mezzanine/temporary equity)</span><span>YES — a distinctly US, SEC-originated presentation tier</span><span>NO equivalent — binary liability-or-equity classification only</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Temporary/mezzanine equity is the single most structurally distinctive feature of US GAAP's liability/equity classification framework — a genuinely American innovation with no IFRS counterpart, forcing IFRS preparers to make a harder binary call that US preparers can sometimes avoid via the intermediate category.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 480 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Mandatorily redeemable instrument classification</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Presented as a liability, not within equity</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Dividends on a liability-classified instrument</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Treated as interest expense</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Mezzanine equity classification</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Presented between liabilities and permanent equity, outside both</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 815-40</span><span>Supplies the detailed indexation and equity classification guidance applying the fixed-for-fixed principle</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 470</span><span>Governs debt instruments once an instrument is classified as a liability under this Topic</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 260</span><span>Earnings Per Share — mezzanine and liability classification affect which instruments participate in EPS computations</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Mandatorily redeemable instrument</div><div class="glossary-def">An instrument embodying an unconditional obligation to redeem by transferring assets at a specified or determinable date, classified as a liability regardless of legal form.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Temporary (mezzanine) equity</div><div class="glossary-def">A US-specific, SEC-originated balance sheet presentation category between liabilities and permanent equity, for instruments redeemable outside the issuer's unconditional control.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Fixed-for-fixed principle</div><div class="glossary-def">The principle that a contract settled by delivering a fixed number of shares for a fixed amount of consideration qualifies for equity classification.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Puttable instrument exception (IFRS)</div><div class="glossary-def">A narrow IAS 32 exception permitting equity classification for certain most-subordinated, pro-rata-sharing puttable instruments, narrower in scope than US mezzanine equity treatment.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc480} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

